  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 1,
    "lesson_title": "Convolution of a Unit Step with a Decaying Exponential",
    "lesson_core_move": "Compute the convolution of a unit step with a decaying exponential by using the causal overlap interval to evaluate the integral and write the result as ((1 - $e^{-at}$)/a)$u(t)$.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 1,
        "lesson_title": "Convolution of a Unit Step with a Decaying Exponential",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Convolution of a Unit Step with a Decaying Exponential",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "This lesson computes a standard causal convolution: a unit step convolved with a decaying exponential. Use the base case $x(t)=u(t)$ and $h(t)=e^{-at}u(t)$, where $a>0$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Start from the convolution integral. In the integral, $x(\\tau)$ becomes $u(\\tau)$, and $h(t-\\tau)$ becomes $e^{-a(t-\\tau)}u(t-\\tau)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}u(\\tau)e^{-a(t-\\tau)}u(t-\\tau)\\,d\\tau",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The two unit steps decide where the integrand is nonzero. The factor $u(\\tau)$ requires $\\tau \\ge 0$. The factor $u(t-\\tau)$ requires $t-\\tau \\ge 0$, which means $\\tau \\le t$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\tau \\ge 0 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\tau \\le t, \\quad \\text{so} \\quad 0 \\le \\tau \\le t \\quad \\text{when } t\\ge 0",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "If $t<0$, those two inequalities do not overlap, so the convolution output is zero. If $t\\ge 0$, the convolution reduces to one exponential integral over the causal overlap interval.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "t\\ge 0:\\quad y(t)=\\int_0^t e^{-a(t-\\tau)}\\,d\\tau=\\frac{1-e^{-at}}{a}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The final unit step records the zero-output region for $t<0$. That gives the reusable result for this lesson.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=\\frac{1-e^{-at}}{a}u(t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 1,
        "lesson_title": "Convolution of a Unit Step with a Decaying Exponential",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Finding the Causal Overlap Interval",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "For $x(t)=u(t)$ and $h(t)=e$^{-2t}$u(t)$, find the values of $\\tau$ where $x(\\tau)$$h(t-\\tau)$ is nonzero.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the pair of unit-step factors in the convolution product. Rewrite the two signals in the convolution variable first.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(\\tau)=u(\\tau),\\qquad h(t-\\tau)=e^{-2(t-\\tau)}u(t-\\tau)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The exponential factor changes the value of the product, but it does not decide where the product is zero. The nonzero interval comes from the two unit steps.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(\\tau)h(t-\\tau)=u(\\tau)e^{-2(t-\\tau)}u(t-\\tau)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The factor $u(\\tau)$ is nonzero only when $\\tau \\ge 0$. The factor $u(t-\\tau)$ is nonzero only when $t-\\tau \\ge 0$, which means $\\tau \\le t$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "u(\\tau):\\ \\tau\\ge 0,\\qquad u(t-\\tau):\\ t-\\tau\\ge 0 \\Longleftrightarrow \\tau\\le t",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now intersect the two conditions. There are $\\tau$ values satisfying both inequalities only when $t \\ge 0$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\begin{cases}0\\le \\tau\\le t, & t\\ge 0,\\\\ \\text{no overlap}, & t<0.\\end{cases}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So $x(\\tau)$$h(t-\\tau)$ is nonzero on the causal overlap interval $0 \\le \\tau \\le t$ when $t \\ge 0$, and there is no nonzero product when $t<0$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 1,
        "lesson_title": "Convolution of a Unit Step with a Decaying Exponential",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Setting Up the Step-Exponential Convolution Integral",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Set up, but do not evaluate, $y(t)=u(t)*e$^{-2t}$u(t)$ as a convolution integral using the causal overlap interval.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the product of two causal factors inside the convolution. Start with the full convolution integral, then use the causal overlap interval to replace the infinite bounds.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}u(\\tau)e^{-2(t-\\tau)}u(t-\\tau)\\,d\\tau",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The factor $u(\\tau)$ requires $\\tau \\ge 0$, and the factor $u(t - \\tau)$ requires $\\tau \\le t$. Together, they leave the interval $0 \\le \\tau \\le t$ when $t \\ge 0$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\tau\\ge 0 \\quad \\text{and}\\quad \\tau\\le t \\quad \\Longrightarrow \\quad 0\\le \\tau\\le t \\quad (t\\ge 0)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "On that interval, the unit-step factors only act as gates, so the integrand left to accumulate is $e^{-2(t - \\tau)}$. Replace the full bounds with the overlap bounds and stop before evaluating.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "t\\ge 0:\\quad y(t)=\\int_0^t e^{-2(t-\\tau)}\\,d\\tau",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "If $t < 0$, the two causal factors have no shared $\\tau$-values where they are both nonzero, so the convolution output is zero in that case.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=\\begin{cases}\\displaystyle\\int_0^t e^{-2(t-\\tau)}\\,d\\tau, & t\\ge 0,\\\\ 0, & t<0.\\end{cases}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 1,
        "lesson_title": "Convolution of a Unit Step with a Decaying Exponential",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Evaluating the Decaying-Exponential Integral",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Evaluate int_0^t $e^{-2(t-\\tau)}$ d $\\tau$ for $t \\ge 0$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the bounded integral from the previous section. Here t is fixed, $\\tau$ is the integration variable, and the job is to evaluate the positive-time branch.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "I(t)=\\int_0^t e^{-2(t-\\tau)}\\,d\\tau,\\qquad t\\ge 0",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Factor: separate the part that depends only on t from the part that depends on $\\tau$. Since $e^{-2t}$ is constant with respect to $\\tau$, it can come outside the integral.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "e^{-2(t-\\tau)}=e^{-2t+2\\tau}=e^{-2t}e^{2\\tau}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "I(t)=e^{-2t}\\int_0^t e^{2\\tau}\\,d\\tau",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Integrate: use the exponential antiderivative with $\\tau$ as the variable.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\int e^{2\\tau}\\,d\\tau=\\frac{1}{2}e^{2\\tau}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Substitute: apply the bounds from 0 to t, then keep the outside factor $e^{-2t}$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "I(t)=e^{-2t}\\left[\\frac{1}{2}e^{2\\tau}\\right]_0^t=e^{-2t}\\left(\\frac{e^{2t}-1}{2}\\right)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Simplify: distribute $e^{-2t}$ through the numerator. This gives the evaluated convolution integral on the overlap region.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "I(t)=\\frac{1-e^{-2t}}{2},\\qquad t\\ge 0",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 1,
        "lesson_title": "Convolution of a Unit Step with a Decaying Exponential",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Writing the Step-Gated Convolution Result",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Write the full convolution result for $y(t)=u(t)*e$^{-2t}$u(t)$, including the zero-output region.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is that the integral has already been evaluated only on the overlap region. First keep that positive-time branch exactly as computed.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "t\\ge 0:\\quad y(t)=\\frac{1-e^{-2t}}{2}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "For $t<0$, the two causal factors have no overlap, so the convolution output is zero.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "t<0:\\quad y(t)=0",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Put those two time regions into one piecewise result before compressing the notation.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=\\begin{cases}\\dfrac{1-e^{-2t}}{2}, & t\\ge 0,\\\\ 0, & t<0.\\end{cases}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The unit step $u(t)$ is the compact way to encode this same zero-output region. Multiplying the positive-time expression by $u(t)$ turns it off for $t<0$ and leaves it active for $t\\ge0$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=\\frac{1-e^{-2t}}{2}u(t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
